Goal: main stages of the water cycle
- [1] - Evaporation, sublimation, and transpiration move water into the atmosphere as vapor from surface water, ice, and plants.
- Condensation cools water vapor at high altitudes into tiny water droplets or ice crystals, forming clouds and fog.
- Precipitation returns condensed water to Earth as rain, snow, drizzle, sleet, or hail.
- Runoff carries water over the surface into rivers, lakes, seas, and oceans, while infiltration soaks it into soil and groundwater.
- [2] - The main stages of the water cycle are evaporation, transpiration, condensation, and precipitation.
- Evaporation happens when the sun heats water in oceans, lakes, and other bodies until it becomes gas and enters the air.
- Transpiration is similar: plant leaves release water vapor into the air.
- Condensation is when water vapor cools and turns back into liquid, forming clouds; precipitation occurs when clouds become too heavy and water falls, restarting the cycle.
- [3] - The water cycle is commonly taught as a simple circular cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.
- A fuller description includes runoff from land and mountains into rivers, infiltration and percolation into soil, and groundwater flow.
- It also includes plant uptake and transpiration, sublimation from ice and snow, and deposition.
- [4] - The most important processes in the water cycle are evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.
- Infiltration and groundwater flow are also shown as part of the cycle.

Goal: why the water cycle matters for life on Earth
- [5] - The water cycle is essential to all life on Earth; without it, life would not exist.
- It regulates climate, supports ecosystems, enables human civilization, and shapes landscapes.
- Water’s ability to exist as solid, liquid, or gas lets the water cycle function as the engine that powers all life.
- [6] - All life on Earth needs water.
- The water cycle changes water between liquid, gas, and solid states.
- Without the water cycle, Earth would not have any fresh water.
- [7] - The water cycle enables water availability for all living organisms.
- It regulates weather patterns on Earth.
- Without natural water recycling, clean water would run out, and clean water is essential to life.
- [8] - Water is vital for life and plays a role in everything from cellular function to climate.
- The water cycle moves water through oceans, atmosphere, land, groundwater, and living things, helping make water available for ecosystems and human activities like agriculture and manufacturing.
- Evaporation absorbs heat and cools Earth’s surface, while condensation releases heat, so the water cycle helps regulate climate.
- Water vapor traps the sun’s heat and creates a greenhouse effect that warms the planet; without it, Earth would be a ball of ice.

Goal: real example of the water cycle being disrupted, e.g. drought
- [9] - Drought-dried land absorbs rainwater poorly, so heavy rain after a drought can run off and cause flash floods.
- Warmer temperatures produce more rain than snow; less snowpack means less meltwater during hot periods.
- Large-scale agricultural groundwater pumping is rapidly depleting aquifers that had stored water for thousands of years.
- Rivers diverted for agriculture can simply dry up, described as the most visible sign of a disrupted water cycle.
- [10] - Persistent drought dropped the Mississippi River to historic lows in 2023, allowing saltwater from the Gulf of Mexico to push upstream and contaminate urban drinking water supplies.
- Drought in Hawaii dried out vegetation and worsened wildfire risk, helping set the stage for the August 2023 Lahaina wildfire that killed 101 people.
- Drought across the Midwest and South from spring to fall became the most costly natural disaster of 2023, at $14.5 billion.
- California’s 2023 atmospheric-river storms and heavy snow relieved drought but barely dented its groundwater deficit; groundwater levels remained lower than after the previous four-year drought ended in 2016.
- [11] - In 2024, severe drought hit the Amazon Basin and other parts of South America, as well as southern Africa.
- Brazil faced simultaneous extremes: catastrophic flooding in the south killed 183 people, while drought in the Amazon continued and affected 59% of Brazil’s territory.
- Only about one-third of global river basins had normal conditions in 2024; the rest had too much or too little water, the sixth consecutive year of imbalance.
- Persistent groundwater deficits continued in parts of Africa, the Americas, and Australia, with only 38% of monitored wells at normal levels.
